        {
          "id": "the_two_HC_speciation_lookups_key_on_the_fuel_subtype_and_on_the_output_pollutant_process_and_serve_four_species",
          "description": "`HCSpeciationCalculator`'s two tables, and the two different things their keys do. `methanethcratio` is keyed by (processID, fuelSubtypeID, regClassID) and a model-year band, and it fires on EVERY rate row of this run because all twenty-six share process 1 -- what selects the methane and NMHC rows is `rt_isMethane` and `rt_isNMHC`, the two pollutants this document names here, because hcspeciation.rs names 5 and 79 as constants too; methane takes the ratio and NMHC its complement, out of the same row. `hcspeciation` is keyed by the OUTPUT pollutant-process, so joining a rate row's own `polProcessID` to it both finds the constants AND identifies which rows are NMOG and VOC rows: neither 80 nor 87 is written anywhere in this document, and the table carries 8001 and 8701 where `process-airtoxics`'s carried 8701 alone, so NMOG needed no new equation at all. Both lookups are INNER, and fuel subtype 90 is in neither table -- that single absence is every species block's twenty missing cohorts. `oxySpeciation` is 0 on all 272 rows, so `speciation_factor` never leaves its constant.",
