          "id": "atmospheric_co2_and_co2_equivalent_are_two_ORDERED_steps_of_one_calculator",
          "description": "`CO2AERunningStartExtendedIdleCalculator`, both steps, and the ORDER between them is the point. Step 1a turns Total Energy into Atmospheric CO2; step 2 then reads `MOVESWorkerOutput` back -- INCLUDING the rows step 1a has just inserted -- and sums Atmospheric CO2, methane and N2O weighted by `globalWarmingPotential`. A port that ran step 2 off the energy would get the same three summands' weights and the wrong answer.\n\nTHE CARBON CHEMISTRY IS CHECKED AGAINST MOVES'S OWN INTERMEDIATE. The snapshot carries `carbonoxidationbyfueltype`, which is the calculator's `CarbonOxidationByFuelType` working table, and this document does not read it: `rt_supplyCarbonContent` and `rt_supplyOxidationFraction` are recomputed from `fuelsupply` and `fuelsubtype`, and the four assertions below are that table's four rows -- 0.01982 / 1 on gasoline, 0.02022 / 1 on diesel, 0.0194 / 1 on ethanol and 0 / 0 on electricity.\n\nELECTRICITY IS EMITTED AT EXACTLY ZERO, which is the opposite of SO2's behaviour on the same cohort and is the one place the two stages can be told apart: `carbonContent` and `oxidationFraction` are REAL zeros rather than absent rows, so `rt_hasCo2Factor` is 1, the row exists, and the Atmospheric CO2 block is 125 cohorts where SO2's is 104. All 42 of those rows are zero and so are their CO2 Equivalent twins, because electricity has no methane row either.\n\nThe worked example: model year 2010 gasoline on the weekday. 1.13466e+07 kJ x 0.01982 x 1 x 44/12 = 824,529 g of CO2; then 1 x 824,529 + 28 x 20.5384 + 265 x 1.43409 = 825,484 g of CO2 Equivalent, which is MOVESOutput's figure to all six digits it stores. The methane and N2O terms are 0.07 % and 0.05 % of the total, so the sum is checked against the reference and each summand separately -- a CO2 Equivalent that had dropped both would still be within 0.12 % of the right answer.\n\nASSERTION BY ASSERTION.\n[1] rt_supplyCarbonContent: `carbonoxidationbyfueltype.sumcarbonContent` for fuel type 1, recomputed. Reference intermediate.\n[2] rt_supplyCarbonContent: Fuel type 2's row of the same table.\n[3] rt_supplyCarbonContent: Fuel type 5's.\n[4] rt_supplyCarbonContent: Fuel type 9's -- a REAL zero, not a missing row.\n[7] rt_co2Factor: 0.01982 x 1 x 44/12, on the EXACT ratio. THE PORT'S OWN ARITHMETIC. The MariaDB decimal 3.6667 would give 0.07268, and the whole of the difference is docs/chain-so2-co2e-mechanism.md 7.1's measurement.\n[8] rt_hasCo2Factor: The electricity CO2 row EXISTS, at zero. Contrast `rt_hasSo2Factor` at the same cohort.\n[9] pol_co2EquivalentWeight: Atmospheric CO2's globalWarmingPotential.\n[10] pol_co2EquivalentWeight: Methane's.\n[11] pol_co2EquivalentWeight: N2O's.\n[12] pol_co2EquivalentWeight: CO2 Equivalent's OWN globalWarmingPotential is NULL, so its weight is 0 and step 2 does not sum itself. This is the assertion that checks the NaN guard: a flag-times-value spelling would have produced 0 x NaN = NaN here.\n[13] out_emissionQuant: Atmospheric CO2, model year 2010 gasoline, weekday. MOVESOutput.\n[14] out_emissionQuant: Atmospheric CO2, model year 2010 electricity: emitted, and zero.\n[15] out_emissionQuant: CO2 Equivalent for the same gasoline cell. MOVESOutput.\n[16] out_emissionQuant: CO2 Equivalent, electricity.\n[17] out_emissionQuant: The methane summand of that CO2 Equivalent. MOVESOutput.\n[18] out_emissionQuant: The N2O summand. MOVESOutput.",
